rted":"","mixed_genotypes":true,"quotes":["4 different missense mutations, p.Asp263His (family 3), p.Arg300Trp (families 2, 4, 5, 11), p.Ile322Thr (family 10), p.Leu401Pro (family 8); 1 nonsense mutation, p.Tyr365* (family 6); and 2 single-base deletions leading to a frameshift and premature stop codon, p.Leu386Argfs*21 (family 7) and p.Asn235Metfs*11 (family 9)","Typical LDS findings such as hypertelorism, bifid uvula and cleft palate, cervical spine instability, and club foot deformity are commonly observed","Other recurrent features include dolichocephaly, high-arched palate, retrognathia (with surgery in case 3-III:1), tall stature, joint hypermobility, arachnodactyly, pectus deformity, and inguinal hernia (Table 1, Figure 2). No evidence for ectopia lentis was found in the medical records."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"heterozygous de novo variant"},{"id":"R:26184463:1","pmid":"26184463","label":"Family with father and two affected children","kind":"family","family":"","n_affected":3,"n_families":1,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"tall stature","hpo_label":"Tall stature","hpo_id":"HP:0000098","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"arachnodactyly","hpo_label":"Arachnodactyly","hpo_id":"HP:0001166","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"hyperextensible joints","hpo_label":"Joint hypermobility","hpo_id":"HP:0001382","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"hypertelorism","hpo_label":"Hypertelorism","hpo_id":"HP:0000316","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"bifid uvula","hpo_label":"Bifid uvula","hpo_id":"HP:0000193","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"cardiac involvement","hpo_label":"Abnormality of the cardiovascular system","hpo_id":"HP:0001626","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"aortic dilation","hpo_label":"Aortic root aneurysm","hpo_id":"HP:0002616","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"eye involvement","hpo_label":"Abnormality of the eye","hpo_id":"HP:0000478","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"TGFB3","as_reported":"c.898C>G, p.Arg300Gly","cdna":"c.898C>G","protein":"p.Arg300Gly","genomic":"","zygosity":"heterozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"missense","key":"V13"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"Sanger sequencing confirmed segregation with the phenotype in the father and two affected children","inheritance":"AD","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["The two affected children and their affected father had tall stature, arachnodactyly, hyperextensible joints, hypertelorism, bifid uvula, but no cardiac involvement, aortic dilation or eye involvement.","We detected a novel heterozygous mutation in TGFB3, c.898C>G, predicting the missense substitution p.Arg300Gly. Sanger sequencing confirmed the mutation and its segregation with the phenotype."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"single heterozygous variant; mode as stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:26184463:1","pmid":"26184463","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Kuechler et al. (2015) suggested that mutations at codon Arg300 of TGFB3 presumably lead to increased TGF-beta signaling, and that the short or tall stature seen in patients with TGFB3 mutations may result from opposing effects of mutations on TGF-beta signaling, contrasting a loss-of-function mutation (p.Cys409Tyr) in an individual with growth retardation with Arg300 mutations in individuals with overgrowth/tall stature.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["The mutations at codon Arg300 presumably lead to increased TGF-beta signalling, suggesting that the short or tall stature seen in patients with TGFB3 mutations may result from opposing effects of mutations on TGF-beta signalling."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:26184463:2","pmid":"26184463","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Kuechler et al. (2015) performed whole exome sequencing in 2 of 3 affected members of a family with features overlapping Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes and identified a heterozygous TGFB3 missense mutation (p.Arg300Gly) at the same codon as a previously reported mutation (p.Arg300Gln) in a child with overgrowth.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["We performed whole exome sequencing in two of three affected individuals from a family with phenotypic features overlapping MFS and LDS.","a child with overgrowth carried a mutation in the same codon as the mutation identified in the three affected individuals reported here (c.899G>A; p.Arg300Gln; Matyas et al., 2014)"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:1","pmid":"25835445","section":"mapping","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) performed linkage analysis in a large Dutch family with syndromic aortic aneurysms and identified 2 large regions on chromosomes 14 and 15 shared by all affected members; the TGFB3 gene on 14q24 was the most prominent candidate.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Linkage analysis using SNP genotypes from 6 patients of the family identified 2 large genomic regions on chromosomes 14 and 15 shared by all affected patients (Online Figure 1). Detailed inspection of the genes in the regions identified several candidates, most prominently the TGFB3 gene on 14q24."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:2","pmid":"25835445","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) screened TGFB3 in 470 probands with syndromic or nonsyndromic thoracic aortic aneurysm and dissection and identified heterozygous TGFB3 mutations in 10 probands in addition to the linkage family, including missense, nonsense, and frameshift mutations; all were absent from control databases and one occurred de novo.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["This revealed additional heterozygous TGFB3 mutations in 10 other probands (7 from Sanger sequencing and 3 from the exome sequencing cohort)","The causal nature of the TGFB3 mutations was further supported by de novo occurrence (family 6) (Figure 1) and absence from controls (all mutations) in EVS, 1000Genomes, and the Genome of the Netherlands."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:3","pmid":"25835445","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) summarized 43 patients (23 males, 20 females) from 11 families with TGFB3 mutations: type A dissection occurred in 4 (median age 51 years), type B dissection in 6 (median age 44.5 years), and aortic aneurysm in 6; skeletal findings included tall stature (12), arachnodactyly (16), pectus deformity (8), kyphoscoliosis (11), and joint hypermobility (9), and LDS features included hypertelorism (14), bifid uvula (11), and cleft palate (5). Not all patients were evaluated for all features.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Type A dissections, age, yrs | 4 (51; 40–80)","Tall stature | 12 Arachnodactyly | 16 Pectus deformity | 8 Kyphoscoliosis | 11 Joint hypermobility | 9","Hypertelorism | 14 Bifid uvula | 11 Cleft palate | 5"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:4","pmid":"25835445","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) observed striking intra- and interfamilial variability, with no early arterial dissection or dissection at small aortic dimension and no striking arterial tortuosity; vascular involvement ranged from none at age 64 to type A or B dissection and death from cerebral aneurysm dissection.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["We observed a striking intrafamilial and interfamilial clinical variability with typical LDS features in some, but complete absence in others.","So far, no examples of early arterial dissection or dissection at small aortic dimension were observed.","No striking aortic or arterial tortuosity was observed."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:5","pmid":"25835445","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) found increased pSMAD2, pERK, and TGFB1 mRNA in aortic wall from a patient with the p.Asp263His mutation, indicating paradoxically enhanced canonical and noncanonical TGF-beta signaling, although the truncating and splice mutations suggested loss of function of TGFB3.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["we observed evidence of paradoxically enhanced TGF-β signaling in the aortic wall of a TGFB3 mutant patient, as shown by increased pSMAD2 (canonical TGF-β signaling), pERK (noncanonical TGF-β signaling), and elevated TGFB1 messenger RNA","Because we observed 3 truncating mutations and an in-frame splice site mutation, we hypothesize that the TGFB3 mutations lead to loss of function (LOF) of TGFB3."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:6","pmid":"25835445","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) noted that TGFB3 mutations affecting residue Arg300 were associated with cleft palate and/or bifid uvula.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["TGFB3 mutations affecting residue Arg300 are associated with cleft palate and/or bifid uvula in our patients (Online Tables 3, 4, and 6) and in the patient reported by Matyas et al. (17)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:25835445:7","pmid":"25835445","section":"clinical_management","statement":"Bertoli-Avella et al. (2015) recommended yearly echocardiographic evaluation of the aortic root in all TGFB3 mutation carriers, with at least 1 baseline imaging of the complete aorta and side branches, and possibly brain vessel imaging depending on family history.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["We recommend yearly echocardiographic evaluation of the aortic root in all mutation carriers, complemented with at least 1 baseline imaging of the complete aorta and side branches."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:23824657:1","pmid":"23824657","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"In a girl with distal arthrogryposis, hypotonia, bifid uvula, hypertelorism, low muscle mass and growth retardation, Rienhoff et al. (2013) used exome sequencing to identify a de novo heterozygous c.1226G>A transition in the TGFB3 gene, which changes cys409 to tyr (C409Y). The patient also carried a de novo CDH2 nonsense mutation (Q70X), which the authors considered unlikely to drive the phenotype.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["A second de novo mutation was found in TGFB3 (c.1226G>A; pC409Y). Validation by Sanger sequencing confirmed that this mutation was de novo in the proband.","We conclude that the proband's mutation in CDH2 (c.208C>T) is unlikely to be driving the clinical phenotype, though we cannot exclude some modifying effects."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23824657:2","pmid":"23824657","section":"gene_function","statement":"Rienhoff et al. (2013) found that the TGFB3 C409Y mutant ligand did not activate a TGF-beta reporter in cultured cells. When coexpressed with wildtype TGFB3 in Xenopus embryos, the mutant reduced pSMAD2 and pERK1/2 signaling to about 40% and 60% of wildtype, consistent with a dominant-negative effect, and it did not affect BMP-induced SMAD1 phosphorylation. The authors concluded that the allele is hypomorphic.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Figure 3 shows that the wild-type (w-t) TGFB3 gene generated a transcriptional TGF-β signal, whereas the TGFB3G1226A gene construct did not. We conclude that the mutant allele encodes a TGFB3 ligand that is not functional.","A 1:1 ratio of mutant-to-wild-type TGFB3 RNA diminished the pSMAD2 and pERK1/2 signals to approximately 40% and 60%, respectively, of that generated by the w-t TGFB3 RNA alone.","We conclude that in the frog system the mutant TGFB3G1226A allele, when co-expressed with w-t TGFB3, has a dominant negative effect on TGF-β signaling measured by pSMAD2 and pERK1/2, but does not affect signaling by BMP2/4/7 ligands measured by pSMAD1."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23824657:3","pmid":"23824657","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Rienhoff et al. (2013) noted that cys409 is the penultimate cysteine of the cysteine knot. This residue is conserved in all TGF-beta family ligands and is essential for ligand activity.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["This penultimate cysteine is conserved in all TGFB family member ligands including orthologues in Caenorhabditis elegans and Drosophila melanogaster defining a structure called the “cysteine knot”, a conformation essential for normal ligand activity"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:23824657:4","pmid":"23824657","section":"clinical_management","statement":"Rienhoff et al. (2013) reported that a 3-year trial of losartan produced no change in muscle strength or mass in their patient. They took this as further evidence of a pathophysiology distinct from that of mutations that enhance TGF-beta signaling.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["At age 3 years, a 3-year trial of losartan at a dose of up to 2.0 mg/kg/day produced no change in muscle strength or mass","The proband had no change in strength or muscle mass after a significant therapeutic trial of losartan, further suggesting a pathophysiology distinct from mutations enhancing TGF-β signaling."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23824657:5","pmid":"23824657","section":"diagnosis","statement":"Rienhoff et al. (2013) found that their patient did not meet diagnostic criteria for Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, or Beals-Hecht syndrome. Distinguishing features included significant growth retardation, absence of cardiovascular findings, and a muscle biopsy that lacked dystrophic changes. No mutations were found in TGFB2, TGFBR1, TGFBR2, SMAD3, FBN1, or FBN2.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The proposita did not meet the diagnostic criteria established for MFS, BHS or LDS. Among the inconsistencies with these diagnoses were significant growth retardation, the absence of cardiovascular findings, and the distinct muscle histopathology.","Analysis of the six genes (TGFB2, TGFBR1, TGFBR2, SMAD3, FBN1, FBN2) associated with MFS, LDS, or BHS identified no mutation."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["TGFB3","SMAD3"],"confirmed":["TGFB3"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":["SMAD3"]},"candidate_inheritance":["AD"]}